 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Quartic Box-Spline on BCC Lattice
-A raycasting method using a 7-direction quartic box-spline on the Body-Centered Cubic (BCC) lattice [S2, unofficial]. It computes the floor of the input, determines a parity vector, computes an origin offset and rotation vector, transforms the local coordinate, and determines the tetrahedron index based on component comparisons [S2, unofficial]. It uses boolean vectors for permutation and computes transformed local coordinates and rotation vectors before fetching samples using axis-aligned updates [S2, unofficial]. The parity is defined as $\pi := \lfloor x \rfloor \mod 2$ [S2, unofficial]. Compared to [Finkbeiner et al. 2010], it is 1.2x faster, and compared to [Csébfalvi and Hadwiger 2006], it is 0.4x slower (i.e., slower) [S2, unofficial].
+A raycasting method using a 7-direction quartic box-spline on the Body-Centered Cubic (BCC) lattice [S2, unofficial]. It computes parity vectors and rotation vectors to transform local coordinates, determining tetrahedron indices via component comparisons. It fetches samples using axis-aligned updates to the origin based on permutation vectors. Compared to the BCC method by Finkbeiner et al., it is 1.2x faster, but 0.4x slower than the Cartesian method by Csébfalvi and Hadwiger [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The evaluation is $O(1)$ per sample due to the fixed stencil size (19 fetches) and branchless transformation [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** The data is stored in a 3D texture with 4 channels, increasing memory usage by 4x compared to a single-channel Cartesian grid of the same physical dimensions, but the FCC lattice requires fewer samples for the same reconstruction quality [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Achieves 20–70 fps for interactive isosurface generation on datasets up to $128 \times 128 \times 256$ [S1]. It is 2.4x faster than BCC raycasting [Finkbeiner et al. 2010] and 0.4x the speed of Cartesian raycasting [Sigg & Hadwiger 2005] [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** High-quality C1 continuous isosurfaces; efficient GPU implementation with no conditional branches in the evaluation loop; symmetric indexing improves cache coherency compared to BCC [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Slower than Cartesian raycasting due to the complexity of the spline evaluation and texture fetch overhead; pre-processing (quasi-interpolation) is required to convert raw data to the spline coefficients [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves interactive speeds of 20–70 fps for isosurface generation on datasets like 128x128x128 [S1].
+*   **Speedup:** Pre-processing (quasi-interpolation and min/max cell construction) is 20 to 30-fold faster using OpenCL kernels [S1].
+*   **Comparison:** On FCC datasets, it is 2.4x faster than the BCC raycaster by Finkbeiner et al., but 0.4x slower (i.e., slower) than the Cartesian lattice method by Sigg & Hadwiger [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Storing FCC data in a four-channel 3D texture is cache-friendly compared to splitting into four Cartesian lattices, but requires channel selection which can slow down performance if not optimized [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast, accurate, and stable evaluation; efficient empty-space skipping; high-quality isosurfaces due to C1 continuity of the box-spline [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Slower than Cartesian methods; channel selection overhead in GPU memory access [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Minho Kim in 2012 [S1]. It builds on the theory of box-splines on non-Cartesian lattices, specifically the six-direction cubic box-spline on the FCC lattice proposed by Entezari and Kim et al. [S1]. The FCC lattice is noted for its optimality in low-rate sampling scenarios [S1]. The method improves upon previous BCC raycasters by providing a symmetric and cache-friendly indexing scheme [S1][S2, unofficial].
+The method was proposed by Minho Kim in 2012 [S1]. It builds on the theory of box-splines on non-Cartesian lattices, specifically the six-direction cubic box-spline on the FCC lattice first proposed by Entezari and later investigated by Kim et al. [S1]. The FCC lattice is optimal for low-rate sampling, offering better sampling efficiency than the Cartesian lattice [S1]. The method addresses the lack of GPU raycasters for FCC datasets at the time, providing a practical reconstruction scheme that balances quality and performance [S1].
 
